# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever cloned the repository.
#
# This matters more than it looks now that CI runs on Windows as well as Linux
# and macOS. Git's default on Windows checks out CRLF, which quietly breaks two
# kinds of file in this tree:
#
#   - Anything compared against generated content. `scripts/check-metrics.ts`
#     writes schemas/metrics.json with LF and reads it back; under CRLF it never
#     matches itself and reports a correct file as stale, on every Windows
#     machine, forever.
#   - Anything with a shebang. A shell script checked out with CRLF fails on a
#     POSIX host with a `bad interpreter` error naming a line that looks fine.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository and checks out LF
# everywhere. The two Windows-only files are the deliberate exceptions: a `.cmd`
# or `.ps1` with LF endings is not reliably executed by cmd.exe.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never tries to translate anything inside them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.apk binary
*.zip binary
*.gguf binary
*.tflite binary
*.mlmodelc binary
